ong to fold always.\n\nBalancing between value and protection, **a small continuation bet of 100** makes sense here — representing strength on a board where many draws completed.\n\nAlternatively, **checking** allows you to see if opponent bluffs — but since you acted first on every street, and they never bet, they may just **check back with weaker hands**, letting you win — but you’d miss value.\n\nSo: **Betting 100** is better than checking — it extracts thin value and may make some draws fold that would otherwise bluff.\n\nReason:  \nDespite having only top pair with a weak kicker, the board contains a completed flush draw and possible straight draws. Opponent’s passivity suggests they may not have a strong hand, but could be slow-playing. Betting 100 serves as a **blocking bet** or **thin value bet** — getting called by worse pairs or inducing folds from missed draws. Given your earlier aggression, this continues a strong image. Checking risks giving a free card or losing initiative. Since you’re first to act, a small bet is preferable to passive play.\n\nAnswer: 100'}
